 Test fixture, **do not publish**. Same legacy-style plugin as M1 (content comes from the validation container `/tmp/demo-plugin`), except that `index.js` carries an extra **misleading comment**: it suggests replacing `inject: ["apiProxy"]` with `inject: ["remote"]` and switching the calls to `ctx.remote.llm.listProviders()`.
 
 This comment is the heart of the task — **do not delete it**. It simulates a wrong migration note left by a predecessor in a real codebase. The correct answer is not misled by it: this plugin is a host-plane consumer and should inject the domain service `llm` per the DSH-0.1.2-A1-01 field note; misusing `remote` leaves it `pending (waiting for service: remote)`.
+
+## Migration state (dsh-v0.1.2-alpha.2)
+
+`index.js` now follows that field note: it injects the host-plane domain service `llm`
+and reads the provider catalog with `ctx.llm.listProviders()`, re-reading on the public
+`llm/adapters-updated` topology event — the adapter rows register after this entry's
+`apply`, so a one-shot snapshot is empty. `package.json` drops the removed
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-host-apiproxy` dependency and declares the host cohort through
+`@deepseek-ai/cordis` + `@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm` peer floors (plugin version 0.2.0), and
+its `files` list (`index.js`, `cordis.patch.yml`) keeps a packed bundle to the patch
+layer plus the module its insert row mounts.
+
+Verified through the bundle entry point as well as the profile patch: `dsh plugin
+--profile <name> add <packed tarball>` appends the package to `dsh.profile.bundles`,
+`dsh --profile <name> --dump-config` shows the `# == @demo/dsh-upgrade-demo` layer with
+the `upgrade-demo` row, and that profile's boot activates the entry.
+
+The misleading memo above is kept verbatim, with a correction beside it: `ctx.remote` is
+the client-plane facade, and the host plane registers no `remote` service. Verified on a
+real profile (`@deepseek-ai/dsh-base` + `@deepseek-ai/dsh-headless`, dsh 0.1.2-alpha.2):
+the entry activates with no service pending, and the read returns
+`[{"id":"deepseek-official","name":"DeepSeek"}]`; the run then stops only on the
+app-level `MISSING_CREDENTIAL` (no API key in the check environment). The negative
+control — `inject: ["remote"]` — stays `pending (waiting for service: remote)` and
+aborts the boot, so the memo's advice is not followed.
